### What happens?

`join_elimination` removes a LEFT JOIN that sits below a `LIMIT ... OFFSET` when the query above only needs DISTINCT values of a left-side column. The join does not change the set of left-side values, but it does change how many rows there are, so it changes which rows the LIMIT/OFFSET keeps. The result is wrong rows, silently.

In the query below the left join duplicates `p` row 6 (it has two matches in `k`). Sorted by `g DESC, id`, the joined rows are `(7,z) (2,y) (4,y) (6,y) (6,y) (1,x) ...`, so `LIMIT 2 OFFSET 3` keeps `(6,y) (6,y)` and the DISTINCT is `y`. With the join eliminated the rows are `(7,z) (2,y) (4,y) (6,y) (1,x) ...`, the slice keeps `(6,y) (1,x)`, and DuckDB answers `x, y`.

The slice alone is right (`[(6, 'y'), (6, 'y')]`); the fault appears when the DISTINCT is put over it. It is right with `SET disabled_optimizers = 'join_elimination'`, with the slice in a `MATERIALIZED` CTE, with a GROUP BY or `count(DISTINCT ...)` in place of the DISTINCT, or when a column of `k` is read.

Related but not the same, as far as I can tell: #25213 (same optimizer, a distinct group kept across UNNEST, fixed by #25552) and #24542 (a filter over LIMIT with LEFT JOIN, on main only; this one is in 1.5.2 and 1.5.6).

### To Reproduce

```sql
CREATE TABLE p (id BIGINT, g VARCHAR);
INSERT INTO p VALUES (1,'x'),(2,'y'),(3,'x'),(4,'y'),(5,'x'),(6,'y'),(7,'z');
CREATE TABLE k (a BIGINT, b BIGINT);
INSERT INTO k VALUES (1,2),(1,3),(2,3),(3,4),(4,1),(5,1),(6,7),(6,4);

-- the slice alone: (6, y), (6, y)
SELECT p.id AS pid, p.g AS pg FROM p LEFT JOIN k ON p.id = k.a
ORDER BY p.g DESC, p.id LIMIT 2 OFFSET 3;

-- expected: y        actual: x, y
SELECT DISTINCT pg FROM (
  SELECT p.id AS pid, p.g AS pg FROM p LEFT JOIN k ON p.id = k.a
  ORDER BY p.g DESC, p.id LIMIT 2 OFFSET 3
) ORDER BY pg;

-- right: y
SET disabled_optimizers = 'join_elimination';
SELECT DISTINCT pg FROM (
  SELECT p.id AS pid, p.g AS pg FROM p LEFT JOIN k ON p.id = k.a
  ORDER BY p.g DESC, p.id LIMIT 2 OFFSET 3
) ORDER BY pg;
SET disabled_optimizers = '';

-- right: y
WITH s AS MATERIALIZED (
  SELECT p.id AS pid, p.g AS pg FROM p LEFT JOIN k ON p.id = k.a
  ORDER BY p.g DESC, p.id LIMIT 2 OFFSET 3
) SELECT DISTINCT pg FROM s ORDER BY pg;
```

### OS:

Linux x86_64

### DuckDB Version:

1.5.2 and 1.5.6; the development build 2.0.0.dev2610011535 shows it too

### DuckDB Client:

Python

### Did you include all relevant data and configuration?

Yes: the two tables are created inline above.

### What happens?

With nested schemas, `COMMENT ON TABLE`, `COMMENT ON VIEW` and `COMMENT ON COLUMN` fail with "Too many qualifications found" as soon as the name has more than three entries (more than four for a column), while `SELECT`, `CREATE TABLE`, `ALTER TABLE` and `CREATE VIEW` accept the same longer paths. A table in `sales.europe` can be commented only without its catalog name, and a table in `sales.europe.north` cannot be commented at all.

### To Reproduce

In the CLI, started without a database file (so the catalog is named `memory`):

```sql
CREATE SCHEMA sales;
CREATE SCHEMA sales.europe;
CREATE SCHEMA sales.europe.north;
CREATE TABLE sales.europe.orders (id INTEGER);
CREATE TABLE sales.europe.north.deep (id INTEGER);
CREATE VIEW sales.europe.v AS SELECT 1 AS id;

-- these succeed
COMMENT ON TABLE sales.europe.orders IS 'x';
COMMENT ON COLUMN sales.europe.orders.id IS 'x';
COMMENT ON VIEW sales.europe.v IS 'x';

-- these fail
COMMENT ON TABLE memory.sales.europe.orders IS 'x';
COMMENT ON TABLE sales.europe.north.deep IS 'x';
COMMENT ON COLUMN sales.europe.north.deep.id IS 'x';
COMMENT ON VIEW memory.sales.europe.v IS 'x';
```

Each failing statement returns:

```
Parser Error: Too many qualifications found - expected [catalog.schema.name] or [schema.name]
```

The error is raised while parsing, so it also appears for names that do not exist, for example `COMMENT ON TABLE a.b.c.d IS 'x'`. I found no workaround for `sales.europe.north.deep`: `USE sales.europe.north` and `SET search_path = 'sales.europe.north'` are refused, so the unqualified name does not resolve either.

The same longer paths work in other statements:

```sql
SELECT * FROM memory.sales.europe.north.deep;
ALTER TABLE memory.sales.europe.north.deep ADD COLUMN extra INTEGER;
CREATE VIEW memory.sales.europe.north.v AS SELECT 1 AS id;
```

In an attached database, a table in `a.b` can be commented only after `USE`, and a table in `a.b.c` cannot be commented at all:

```sql
ATTACH ':memory:' AS other;
CREATE SCHEMA other.a;
CREATE SCHEMA other.a.b;
CREATE SCHEMA other.a.b.c;
CREATE TABLE other.a.b.t (id INTEGER);
CREATE TABLE other.a.b.c.u (id INTEGER);

COMMENT ON TABLE other.a.b.t IS 'x';  -- fails
USE other;
COMMENT ON TABLE a.b.t IS 'x';        -- succeeds
COMMENT ON TABLE a.b.c.u IS 'x';      -- fails
```

### Expected

`COMMENT ON` accepts the same qualified paths as `CREATE TABLE` and `SELECT`: the catalog plus any number of schema levels, for tables, views and columns. The [documentation for nested schemas](https://duckdb.org/docs/2.0/sql/statements/create_schema#nested-schemas) says objects in a nested schema are referenced by their fully qualified, dot-separated path, and the [limitations of `COMMENT ON`](https://duckdb.org/docs/2.0/sql/statements/comment_on#limitations) do not mention nested schemas.

### Where the limit is

`TransformCommentStatement` (`src/parser/peg/transformer/transform_comment.cpp`) passes the dotted name to `PEGTransformerFactory::StringToQualifiedName` (`src/parser/peg/transformer/peg_transformer_factory.cpp`), which throws this error for more than three entries. For `COMMENT ON COLUMN` the column name is split off first, so a column reference can have four. I have not built `main`, but this code is unchanged there (c15a232a57). #25070 removed 3- and 4-level limits elsewhere in the parser but did not touch `comment.gram` or `transform_comment.cpp`. The tests in `test/sql/catalog/nested_schema/` do cover `COMMENT ON` in nested schemas, but only with one level of nesting and no catalog prefix, for example `COMMENT ON TABLE s1.s2.t`. `test/sql/catalog/comment_on_extended.test` expects `COMMENT ON COLUMN galaxy.db.schema.table.col1 IS 'no bueno'` to fail, so a fix needs to change that test.

### Environment

- OS: macOS 26.7.1, arm64
- DuckDB version: v2.0.0 (source id d54cbbe3df)
- DuckDB client: CLI from the v2.0.0 release (`v2.0.0 (Cyanoptera) d54cbbe3df`) and R (duckdb package 2.0.0), with the same result in both
- Full name: Travis Gerke
- No data set is needed.

### What happens?

A filter on a dictionary-encoded Arrow column whose values are `string_view` (`dictionary<values=string_view, indices=int8>`) raises `duckdb.Error` carrying pyarrow's `ArrowNotImplementedError: Function 'equal' has no kernel matching input types (string_view, string)`. The same filter on a plain `string_view` column works, because filters on view types are not pushed down (duckdb/duckdb#20165, for #187), and since duckdb/duckdb#20703 (for #227) a view column turns off pushdown for the whole table. A filter on another column of the same table works too.

Likely cause: `CanPushdown(const ArrowType &type)` in duckdb/duckdb's `src/function/table/arrow.cpp` judges a column by `type.GetDuckType()`, which for a dictionary-encoded column is the index type. An integer index type is always pushable, so the filter is pushed down to pyarrow even though it compares the dictionary's values, and pyarrow can't evaluate the comparison. `HasViewType` also judges by `GetDuckType()` and so never sees a dictionary's values; that is right for pyarrow, whose `array_filter` filters a dictionary column by its indices.

#### Suggested fix

The change is in duckdb/duckdb, where #20165 and #20703 also landed. Judge a dictionary-encoded column by its value type:
```diff
 static bool CanPushdown(const ArrowType &type) {
+	if (type.HasDictionary()) {
+		// a filter on a dictionary-encoded column compares the dictionary's values, not its indices
+		return CanPushdown(type.GetDictionary());
+	}
 	auto duck_type = type.GetDuckType();
```
With this change, `CanPushdown` refuses `dictionary<string_view>`. Dictionary columns whose value types `CanPushdown` already rejects also stop being pushed down. `dictionary<utf8>` is still pushed. A test case for `test/arrow/arrow_filter_pushdown.cpp` is below. It checks the EXPLAIN plan, and its string_view section fails without the change and passes with it. A filter on another column of that table is still pushed. The `[arrow]` tests pass.

<details><summary>Test</summary>

```diff
--- a/test/arrow/arrow_filter_pushdown.cpp
+++ b/test/arrow/arrow_filter_pushdown.cpp
@@ -1,6 +1,8 @@
 #include "catch.hpp"

 #include "arrow/arrow_test_helper.hpp"
+#include "duckdb/common/adbc/single_batch_array_stream.hpp"
+#include "duckdb/common/types/arrow_string_view_type.hpp"

 using namespace duckdb;

@@ -23,8 +25,8 @@
 }

 // Helper: get the EXPLAIN output for an arrow_scan with a filter
-static string GetExplainForFilter(Connection &con, ArrowTestFactory &factory, const string &filter_expr) {
-	const auto params = ArrowTestHelper::ConstructArrowScan(factory);
+static string GetExplainForFilter(Connection &con, const shared_ptr<ArrowScanFactory> &params,
+                                  const string &filter_expr) {
 	const auto rel = con.TableFunction("arrow_scan", {}, {}, params)->Filter(filter_expr);
 	// render operator names raw/upper-case so the string checks below (ARROW_SCAN, FILTER) match
 	REQUIRE(!con.Query("SET profiling_renderer_settings = MAP {'operator_casing': 'upper'}")->HasError());
@@ -34,6 +36,10 @@
 	return mat.Collection().GetValue(1, 0).ToString();
 }

+static string GetExplainForFilter(Connection &con, ArrowTestFactory &factory, const string &filter_expr) {
+	return GetExplainForFilter(con, ArrowTestHelper::ConstructArrowScan(factory), filter_expr);
+}
+
 // Helper: check for a standalone FILTER operator node in the explain output
 static bool StandaloneFilter(const std::string &explain_str) {
 	// Look for FILTER as a standalone operator node name (not "Filters:" which is a scan attribute)
@@ -107,3 +113,142 @@
 		REQUIRE(!FilterInScan(explain_str));
 	}
 }
+
+namespace arrow_filter_pushdown_dict_test {
+
+// Stack-allocated Arrow structs need a no-op release.
+void ReleaseSchema(ArrowSchema *s) {
+	s->release = nullptr;
+}
+void ReleaseArray(ArrowArray *a) {
+	a->release = nullptr;
+}
+
+const int32_t X_VALUES[5] = {0, 1, 2, 3, 4};
+const int8_t D_INDICES[5] = {0, 1, 2, 0, 1};
+const int32_t UTF8_OFFSETS[4] = {0, 3, 8, 12};
+const char UTF8_DATA[] = "redgreenblue";
+// a string view array with no variadic data buffers still ends with the (empty) buffer-sizes buffer
+const int64_t NO_BUFFER_SIZES[1] = {0};
+
+enum class DictValues { UTF8, STRING_VIEW };
+
+// One batch of two columns: x INTEGER 0..4, and d holding "red", "green", "blue", "red", "green" as a dictionary
+// with int8 indices whose values are utf8 or string view, as a single-batch stream for binding the scan.
+struct DictTable {
+	explicit DictTable(DictValues values)
+	    : views {arrow_string_view_t(3, "red"), arrow_string_view_t(5, "green"), arrow_string_view_t(4, "blue")} {
+		dict_buffers[0] = nullptr;
+		switch (values) {
+		case DictValues::UTF8:
+			dict_schema.format = "u";
+			dict_buffers[1] = UTF8_OFFSETS;
+			dict_buffers[2] = UTF8_DATA;
+			break;
+		case DictValues::STRING_VIEW:
+			// every value fits inline, so the views buffer is followed only by the buffer sizes
+			dict_schema.format = "vu";
+			dict_buffers[1] = views;
+			dict_buffers[2] = NO_BUFFER_SIZES;
+			break;
+		}
+		dict_schema.release = ReleaseSchema;
+		dict_array.length = 3;
+		dict_array.n_buffers = 3;
+		dict_array.buffers = dict_buffers;
+		dict_array.release = ReleaseArray;
+
+		x_schema.format = "i";
+		x_schema.name = "x";
+		x_schema.release = ReleaseSchema;
+		x_buffers[0] = nullptr;
+		x_buffers[1] = X_VALUES;
+		x_array.length = 5;
+		x_array.n_buffers = 2;
+		x_array.buffers = x_buffers;
+		x_array.release = ReleaseArray;
+
+		d_schema.format = "c";
+		d_schema.name = "d";
+		d_schema.dictionary = &dict_schema;
+		d_schema.release = ReleaseSchema;
+		d_buffers[0] = nullptr;
+		d_buffers[1] = D_INDICES;
+		d_array.length = 5;
+		d_array.n_buffers = 2;
+		d_array.buffers = d_buffers;
+		d_array.dictionary = &dict_array;
+		d_array.release = ReleaseArray;
+
+		schema_children[0] = &x_schema;
+		schema_children[1] = &d_schema;
+		record_schema.format = "+s";
+		record_schema.n_children = 2;
+		record_schema.children = schema_children;
+		record_schema.release = ReleaseSchema;
+		array_children[0] = &x_array;
+		array_children[1] = &d_array;
+		record_array.length = 5;
+		record_array.n_buffers = 1;
+		record_array.buffers = record_buffers;
+		record_array.n_children = 2;
+		record_array.children = array_children;
+		record_array.release = ReleaseArray;
+		AdbcError err {};
+		REQUIRE(duckdb_adbc::BatchToArrayStream(&record_array, &record_schema, &stream, &err) == ADBC_STATUS_OK);
+	}
+	DictTable(const DictTable &) = delete;
+	~DictTable() {
+		// an EXPLAIN only binds the scan, so the stream is still ours to release
+		if (stream.release) {
+			stream.release(&stream);
+		}
+	}
+
+	arrow_string_view_t views[3];
+	const void *dict_buffers[3];
+	const void *x_buffers[2];
+	const void *d_buffers[2];
+	const void *record_buffers[1] = {nullptr};
+	ArrowSchema *schema_children[2];
+	ArrowArray *array_children[2];
+	ArrowSchema dict_schema {};
+	ArrowSchema x_schema {};
+	ArrowSchema d_schema {};
+	ArrowSchema record_schema {};
+	ArrowArray dict_array {};
+	ArrowArray x_array {};
+	ArrowArray d_array {};
+	ArrowArray record_array {};
+	ArrowArrayStream stream {};
+};
+
+} // namespace arrow_filter_pushdown_dict_test
+
+TEST_CASE("Arrow filter pushdown - dictionary columns are judged by their value type", "[arrow]") {
+	using namespace arrow_filter_pushdown_dict_test;
+	DuckDB db;
+	Connection con(db);
+
+	SECTION("Filter on a dictionary column with string view values: filter above scan (not pushed)") {
+		DictTable table(DictValues::STRING_VIEW);
+		auto explain_str = GetExplainForFilter(con, ArrowTestHelper::ConstructArrowScan(table.stream), "d = 'red'");
+		REQUIRE(StandaloneFilter(explain_str));
+		REQUIRE(!FilterInScan(explain_str));
+	}
+
+	SECTION("Filter on another column of a table with a string view dictionary: filter pushed into scan") {
+		// pyarrow filters a dictionary column by its indices and never reads the values
+		DictTable table(DictValues::STRING_VIEW);
+		auto explain_str = GetExplainForFilter(con, ArrowTestHelper::ConstructArrowScan(table.stream), "x > 2");
+		REQUIRE(!StandaloneFilter(explain_str));
+		REQUIRE(FilterInScan(explain_str));
+	}
+
+	SECTION("Filter on a dictionary column with utf8 values: filter pushed into scan") {
+		DictTable table(DictValues::UTF8);
+		auto explain_str = GetExplainForFilter(con, ArrowTestHelper::ConstructArrowScan(table.stream), "d = 'red'");
+		REQUIRE(!StandaloneFilter(explain_str));
+		REQUIRE(FilterInScan(explain_str));
+	}
+}
```

</details>

### To Reproduce

```python
import duckdb
import pyarrow as pa
import pyarrow.dataset as ds

d = pa.DictionaryArray.from_arrays(
    pa.array([0, 1, 0], pa.int8()), pa.array(["red", "blue"], pa.string_view())
)
c = duckdb.connect()
c.register("t", ds.dataset(pa.table({"x": [1, 2, 3], "d": d})))
print(c.sql("SELECT x FROM t WHERE x > 1").fetchall())    # [(2,), (3,)]
print(c.sql("SELECT x FROM t WHERE d = 'red'").fetchall())  # duckdb.Error: ArrowNotImplementedError: ...
```
Expected: `[(1,), (3,)]` from the second query.

The same failure occurs when `t` is the `pa.Table` itself, a `pa.RecordBatchReader` or a `pyarrow.dataset.Scanner`, and with `duckdb.from_arrow(...)`: any scan whose pushed filters pyarrow evaluates.

